  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Ohio received $5.6B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state logged 47,223 awards with an average award size of $120K. Obligations fell 42.6% year over year. The Department of Defense led buying activity with $3.7B in obligations, followed by the General Services Administration with $453M and the Department of Energy with $450M. GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY, MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP, and SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC are the largest named vendors, with $564M, $452M, and $322M respectively. Research and Development in the Physical, Engineering, and Life Sciences is the largest industry by obligations at $883M. Aircraft Engine and Engine Parts Manufacturing and Petroleum Refineries follow with $501M and $498M. Fiscal-year totals show FY26 at $5.6B compared with $9.8B in FY25 and $9B in FY24, reflecting the current-year totals as partial fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How did federal contract spending in Ohio change year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 42.6% year over year. Ohio recorded $5.6B in obligations in FY26 to date, down from $9.8B in FY25. Fiscal-year obligations show volatility: FY23 was $10.1B, FY24 was $9B, and FY25 was $9.8B."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Who are the biggest buyers and vendors in Ohio?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $3.7B. The General Services Administration obligated $453M and the Department of Energy obligated $450M. GENERAL ELECTRIC COMPANY is the largest vendor with $564M, followed by MARATHON PETROLEUM COMPANY LP with $452M and SOUTHERN OHIO CLEANUP COMPANY LLC with $322M."
      }
    ]
  },
